      {
        "caution_id": "evidence-gap:7a9602bd3355d15efa67cebc",
        "description": "Root identification across versions: The paper requests a user-identified initial cluster. Preserve this edition-specific instruction; do not infer every present software version must receive an explicit root argument.",
        "evidence": [
          {
            "content_hash": "cc09d24a0aa6d141ec06fe6ee9f1c496276e64fd81221eb6e61e35272bf5515e",
            "end_offset": 44323,
            "evidence_span_id": "evidence-span:73ff360a36efd09c93e58935",
            "exact_text": "Slingshot allows two forms of supervision during lineage identification: initial state and terminal states specification. Like other methods (TSCAN, Waterfall, Monocle 2), Slingshot requires the user to identify the initial cluster or root node. Subsequently, every direct path from this node to a leaf node (i.e., a cluster with only one edge) will be called a lineage. Indeed, all existing lineage inference methods explicitly or implicitly make the assumption that a starting state can be identified by the user: Monocle and Embeddr construct orderings for which the user must select the correct direction and Wishbone and DPT require the user to select an initial cell or group of cells. In the simple case where the MST constructed by Slingshot has only two leaf nodes and one is specified as the root, this results in a single lineage. If an interior (non-leaf) node is specified as the origin, this results in two lineages, one terminating in each leaf node. Clusters with more than two edges will create bifurcations and produce additional lineages.",
            "locator": {
              "kind": "section",
              "value": "source-artifact:7114f7708cd37af0bbb6b872cf9e014b/xml:/article/body/sec[4]/sec[1]/sec[2]/p[1];text-sha256:2e29926f594881988dbe8b4140268a40f9824cc2c7c1597ab5d78aac1d839a70:43266:44323"
